  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nFriday evening reveal southwesterly flow is in place across the\ncentral Rockies/plains between a strong cut-off low centered \nover southern Idaho and a ~594-dm ridge downstream over the \nsouthern plains. At the surface, an elongated area of low \npressure extends from east- central CO northeastward through \nnorthwest KS and into south-central NE, separating warm \ntemperatures to the south and cool temperatures to the north. As\nthe upper level low inches eastward daytime Saturday, the \nsurface low will sag southward, allowing a cold front to move \nslowly through southwest KS. Southwesterly downslope flow ahead \nof this boundary will yield one final day of summer-like \ntemperatures with highs in the mid/upper 90s for roughly the \nsoutheastern half of our CWA while the northwestern half begins \nto cool off within the post-frontal northerlies with highs in \nthe mid 80s to low 90s. This cold front may also serve as the \nfocus for afternoon thunderstorm development, both along/ahead \nof it as well as within the post-frontal upslope flow regime in \nsoutheast CO. Any convection that develops along the boundary or\nmeanders into our area from southeast CO will carry a marginal \nsevere threat given the presence of around 1000 J/Kg of MLCAPE \nand ~30 kts of deep layer shear. The primary threats associated \nwith this activity will be large hail and damaging wind gusts. \nElevated convection may persist into the overnight period \nsustained by 850-700-mb warm advection, but these will pose a \ndecreasing severe threat with time. Otherwise, cold advection \nwill promote Sunday morning lows dropping into the upper 50s/low\n60s northwest to upper 60s southeast.\n\nDaytime Sunday, the upper level trough will eject onto the \nnorthern plains as surface high pressure settles into the \ncentral plains behind the cold front, and at long last, most of \nsouthwest KS will enjoy cool temperatures as afternoon highs \npeak in the 70s. Some areas along/near the KS/OK border may \nstill reach into the low to mid 80s owing to a more shallow cool\nairmass, but this will still be a welcome change from the upper\n90s/near 100 heat these areas have been mired in for months.\n\nMonday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nagree the cool temperatures are here to stay as NBM highs \nremain in the 70s to mid-80s range through at least the end of \nthe business week. Ensembles also suggest that precipitation \nchances will exist most, if not all days, favoring our western \nzones. Highest probability of widespread precipitation currently\nappears to surround Thursday evening and overnight as the NBM \npaints chance pops (25-54%) across nearly our entire area.",
